From 74d85fa0bbe8a80f1b891ddb606c6ad1fe400449 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Fran=C3=A7ois=20Roux?= <info@humanlearning.ch>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 11:01:14 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] remoteproc: qcom_q6v5_pas: Disable recovery when there is no
 .start
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

qcom_pas_ops_no_reset is selected by "qcom,broken-reset" and provides only
.attach, .da_to_va, .stop and .panic. There is no .start, because such a
remoteproc was brought up by the boot firmware and the kernel only ever
attaches to it.

rproc_trigger_recovery() does not know that. It picks rproc_boot_recovery(),
which reaches remoteproc_core.c and calls rproc->ops->start(rproc) with no
NULL check. The recovery worker dies on the spot.

Observed on a Microsoft Surface Pro 12in (X1P42100) booted at EL2 via
slbounce, where the ADSP and CDSP are attached rather than started. An
unprompted CDSP crash produced:

	fatal error received: sleep_statsi.c:537
	Unable to handle kernel NULL pointer dereference at virtual address 0
	pc : 0x0
	lr : rproc_start+0xc0

and left the CDSP offline until the next reboot. The ADSP was untouched, so
audio survived; an ADSP crash would have cost sound the same way.

Recovery cannot work without .start in any case, so refuse it rather than
crash trying. The condition is on ops->start rather than on the device-tree
property, so any future ops set lacking .start is covered too -- the same
test is already used two lines above to pick the auto_boot mode.

The crash is reproducible on demand via debugfs:

	echo 1 > /sys/kernel/debug/remoteproc/remoteproc1/crash

Reported to Stephan Gerhold, who suggested this fix over the
RPROC_FEAT_ATTACH_ON_RECOVERY approach first proposed.

Not sent upstream: it applies to the out-of-tree attach series, which is
not merged.

Tool assistance, per Documentation/process/generated-content.rst: the
diff and this commit message were produced in a long interactive session with
an AI coding assistant. The human signer directed the work, ran the crash
experiment and its control on the machine, and reviewed the result. The
verification was empirical, on the hardware described.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: François Roux <info@humanlearning.ch>
---
 drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5_pas.c | 16 ++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5_pas.c b/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5_pas.c
index 89b134b331fb..566296b8dcd0 100644
--- a/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5_pas.c
+++ b/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5_pas.c
@@ -825,6 +825,22 @@ static int qcom_pas_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 	} else {
 		rproc->auto_boot = RPROC_AUTO_BOOT_DISABLED;
 	}
+	/*
+	 * An attach-only remoteproc has no .start: qcom_pas_ops_no_reset is
+	 * selected by "qcom,broken-reset" and provides only .attach, .da_to_va,
+	 * .stop and .panic. rproc_trigger_recovery() nevertheless picks
+	 * rproc_boot_recovery(), which calls rproc->ops->start() with no NULL
+	 * check -- observed on a Surface Pro 12in (X1P42100) booted at EL2,
+	 * where an unprompted CDSP crash took the recovery worker down with a
+	 * NULL pointer dereference at pc 0x0, lr rproc_start+0xc0, and left the
+	 * DSP offline until reboot.
+	 *
+	 * Recovery cannot work without .start anyway, so refuse it outright
+	 * rather than crash trying.
+	 */
+	if (!ops->start)
+		rproc->recovery_disabled = true;
+
 	rproc_coredump_set_elf_info(rproc, ELFCLASS32, EM_NONE);
 
 	pas = rproc->priv;
-- 
